roup":"theme-3","groupLabel":"Theme 3","groupName":"Business Behaviour and the Labour Market","unit":"3.6","unitName":"Government Intervention","notesUrl":"/revision-notes/edexcel-theme-3/3-6-1-government-intervention.html","questionsUrl":"/practice-questions/edexcel-theme-3/3-6-1-government-intervention.html","url":"/past-paper-questions/edexcel/3-6-1-government-intervention/","count":11,"gated":true,"hasPage":true}},"questions":[{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2025-jun-q7","p":6,"section":"C","questionNumber":"7","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":"edexcel-a-p1-2025-jun-sec-c","marks":25,"questionText":"High Speed 2 (HS2) was a £100 billion UK government rail project designed to expand the existing network. The journey time between London and Manchester would take just 1 hour and 11 minutes, 54 minutes quicker than today’s fastest time. Almost 900 homes, 1 000 businesses and around 60 ancient woodlands would be destroyed by its construction. Evaluate the likely microeconomic effects of building major infrastructure projects.","topics":["1-3-2-externalities","1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["HS2","infrastructure","externalities","cost-benefit analysis"],"qpPage":26,"msPage":21,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2024-jun-q4a","p":5,"section":"A","questionNumber":"4(a)","parentQuestion":"4","choiceGroup":null,"marks":4,"questionText":"In 2022 the government announced that the maximum undergraduate tuition fee for universities in England will remain at £9 250 per year for the next two years. Explain one likely reason for the maximum price for an undergraduate university programme.","topics":["1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["maximum price","tuition fees","government intervention"],"qpPage":7,"msPage":10,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2024-jun-q4b","p":5,"section":"A","questionNumber":"4(b)","parentQuestion":"4","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"In 2022 the government announced that the maximum undergraduate tuition fee for universities in England will remain at £9 250 per year for the next two years. Which one of the following is an example of government failure associated with the maximum price?","topics":["1-4-2-government-failure","1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["government failure","maximum price","distortion of price signals","tuition fees"],"qpPage":7,"msPage":11,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["Contraction in demand","Distortion of price signals","Excess supply","Extension in supply"],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"edexcel-as-p1-2024-jun-q6d","p":33,"section":"B","questionNumber":"6(d)","parentQuestion":"6","choiceGroup":null,"marks":6,"questionText":"With reference to Extract C, explain how incentives to use reusable coffee cups may lead to a fall in demand for single‑use cups.","topics":["1-2-2-demand","1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["incentives","demand","reusable cups","nudge"],"qpPage":14,"msPage":15,"ctxPage":12,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2024-jun-q6e","p":5,"section":"B","questionNumber":"6(e)","parentQuestion":"6","choiceGroup":null,"marks":15,"questionText":"With reference to Extract D, discuss possible methods of government intervention to reduce electronic waste.","topics":["1-3-2-externalities","1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["electronic waste","negative externalities","government intervention","recycling"],"qpPage":12,"msPage":21,"ctxPage":10,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-as-p1-2024-jun-q6g","p":33,"section":"B","questionNumber":"6(g)","parentQuestion":"6","choiceGroup":"edexcel-as-p1-2024-jun-q6-choice","marks":20,"questionText":"Evaluate methods of government intervention which could be used to reduce the market failure associated with plastic waste. Use a diagram to support your answer.","topics":["1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets","1-3-2-externalities"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["government intervention","plastic waste","market failure","regulation"],"qpPage":14,"msPage":22,"ctxPage":12,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-as-p1-2023-jun-q1a","p":32,"section":"A","questionNumber":"1(a)","parentQuestion":"1","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"In 2021, energy regulator Ofgem announced that the maximum price of energy will rise from £ 1 277 to £ 1 971 a year, for the average household bill. Which one of the following is the percentage increase in the maximum price to the average household bill?","topics":["1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["maximum price","energy price cap","percentage change","calculation"],"qpPage":2,"msPage":4,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["35 %","45 %","54 %","694 %"],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"edexcel-as-p1-2023-jun-q1b","p":32,"section":"A","questionNumber":"1(b)","parentQuestion":"1","choiceGroup":null,"marks":2,"questionText":"In 2021, energy regulator Ofgem announced that the maximum price of energy will rise from £ 1 277 to £ 1 971 a year, for the average household bill. Annotate the diagram below to show the original and new maximum price in this market.","topics":["1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["maximum price","energy price cap","diagram"],"qpPage":2,"msPage":4,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2023-jun-q4c","p":4,"section":"A","questionNumber":"4(c)","parentQuestion":"4","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"An annual season ticket for a train journey between London and Reading is planned to increase from £5 044 to £5 664 in 2023. The diagram below shows the demand and supply for sugary drinks. Which one of the following areas represents the change in producer surplus when a minimum price is imposed?","topics":["1-2-8-producer-consumer-surplus","1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["producer surplus","minimum price","sugary drinks"],"qpPage":9,"msPage":8,"ctxPage":8,"modelAnswer":null,"ctxLabel":"Question 4 as printed","type":"mcq","options":["FEPeP1","FMP1","GEPeP1","GKP1"],"usesFigure":true},{"id":"edexcel-as-p1-2023-jun-q6f","p":32,"section":"B","questionNumber":"6(f)","parentQuestion":"6","choiceGroup":"edexcel-as-p1-2023-jun-q6-choice","marks":20,"questionText":"Evaluate possible methods of government intervention to reduce the environmental damage caused by the consumption of television content.","topics":["1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets","1-3-2-externalities"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["government intervention","environmental damage","externalities"],"qpPage":13,"msPage":21,"ctxPage":11,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2023-jun-q7","p":4,"section":"C","questionNumber":"7","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":"edexcel-a-p1-2023-jun-sec-c","marks":25,"questionText":"The average UK household energy bill (gas and electric combined) rose 80% from £1 971 in 2021 to £3 549 in 2022. Evaluate possible methods of government intervention to control household energy bills in the UK.","topics":["1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets","3-6-1-government-intervention"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1","theme-3"],"keywords":["energy price cap","government intervention","maximum price","energy bills"],"qpPage":29,"msPage":21,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2023-jun-q8","p":4,"section":"C","questionNumber":"8","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":"edexcel-a-p1-2023-jun-sec-c","marks":25,"questionText":"Using 2021 estimates of carbon emissions, it is estimated that a petrol car journey from London to Glasgow emits approximately 3.3 times more carbon dioxide per passenger than the equivalent journey by train. Evaluate possible methods of government intervention to reduce carbon emissions caused by road transport in the UK.","topics":["1-3-2-externalities","1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["carbon emissions","negative externalities","carbon tax","road transport"],"qpPage":29,"msPage":23,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-as-p1-2022-jun-q2a","p":31,"section":"A","questionNumber":"2(a)","parentQuestion":"2","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"The Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) is a system of minimum prices and other programmes covering farming, environmental measures and rural development. The CAP applied to British farmers before the UK left the EU in January 2020. Define the term ‘minimum price’.","topics":["1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["minimum price","definition"],"qpPage":3,"msPage":5,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-as-p1-2022-jun-q2b","p":31,"section":"A","questionNumber":"2(b)","parentQuestion":"2","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"The Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) is a system of minimum prices and other programmes covering farming, environmental measures and rural development. The CAP applied to British farmers before the UK left the EU in January 2020. The diagram below shows the demand and supply curves for wheat in the European Union (EU). The free market price of wheat is P1. It had been agreed as part of the CAP to maintain a price of P2 within the EU and to do so through intervention buying. In achieving a price of P2 , the amount spent on intervention buying by the EU would be","topics":["1-4-1-government-intervention-in-markets","1-2-6-price-determination"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1"],"keywords":["minimum price","intervention buying","Common Agricultural Policy","buffer stocks"],"qpPage":3,"msPage":5,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type